<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">S. W. R. D. Bandaranaike</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a>–<a href="/years/1959">1959</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">S. W. R. D. Bandaranaike was Prime Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> from 1956 until his assassination in 1959. He founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> Freedom Party in 1951 and won the 1956 election on a Sinhalese nationalist programme, passing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinhala-only-act">Sinhala Only Act</a>, which made Sinhala the sole official language. He was shot by a Buddhist monk, and his widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirimavo-bandaranaike">Sirimavo Bandaranaike</a> became the world's first woman prime minister in 1960.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/s-w-r-d-bandaranaike.jpg" alt="S. W. R. D. Bandaranaike" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This image is a work of an employee of the Government of Ceylon (Sri Lanka) / Scanned Edited &amp; Uploaded By MediaJet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Official_Photographic_Portrait_of_S.W.R.D.Bandaranayaka_(1899-1959).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Solomon West Ridgeway Dias Bandaranaike was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a> on 8 January 1899 into a wealthy Sinhalese Anglican family that was among the more elite native families under British rule. His father was Sir Solomon Dias Bandaranaike, Maha Mudaliyar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>. He was tutored at home, attended St Thomas' College, Mutwal, and read philosophy, politics and economics at Christ Church, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, graduating with honours in 1923. A keen debater at the Oxford Union, he came third in the contest for its presidency. Called to the bar at the Inner Temple in 1924, he became an advocate of the Supreme Court of Ceylon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Bandaranaike became secretary of the Ceylon National Congress in 1926 and was elected to the Colombo Municipal Council that year. He was elected unopposed to the first State Council in 1931, became Minister of Local Administration in 1936 and founded the Sinhala Maha Sabha that year to promote Sinhalese culture and community interests.</p><p>The Sinhala Maha Sabha merged into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-national-party">United National Party</a> in 1947, and he became Minister of Health and Local Government and Leader of the House under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/d-s-senanayake">D. S. Senanayake</a>. In July 1951, at odds with the government, he crossed the floor and founded the Sri Lanka Freedom Party, whose inaugural meeting was held on 2 September 1951. He built its support in rural areas around the 'Pancha Maha Balavegaya', or Five Great Forces: native doctors, clergy, teachers, farmers and workers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>For the 1956 election he assembled the Mahajana Eksath Peramuna, a four-party coalition with Marxist parties, which won a landslide with a two-thirds majority, and he became fourth Prime Minister in April 1956. The Official Language Act of 1956, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinhala-only-act">Sinhala Only Act</a>, made Sinhala the sole official language in place of English, a step that many Sinhalese saw as asserting national identity after colonial rule. It created distrust among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lankan-tamils">Sri Lankan Tamils</a> and was followed by riots in 1956 and 1958. He signed the Bandaranaike-Chelvanayakam Pact in 1957 but withdrew it in 1958 under pressure from within his party.</p><p>His government nationalised the bus services, introduced legislation prohibiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>-based discrimination and abrogated the 1947 United Kingdom-Ceylon Defence Agreement, which led to the withdrawal of British forces from their bases. He was shot at his town house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a> on 25 September 1959 and died the next day, and a Buddhist monk, Talduwe Somarama, was convicted and hanged. His widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirimavo-bandaranaike">Sirimavo Bandaranaike</a> led the Freedom Party to a majority and became the world's first woman prime minister.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “S. W. R. D. Bandaranaike” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>S. W. R. D. Bandaranaike</b> won the 1956 election and passed the Sinhala Only Act, deepening tension with Tamils.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominion-of-ceylon">Dominion of Ceylon</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=6.93,79.86&amp;year=1938" aria-label="Colombo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of S. W. R. D. Bandaranaike"><image href="/maps/globes/g09000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4144 4409h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4144 4409h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=6.93,79.86&amp;year=1938">What was here in 1938? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
